[2C[7A[?25h[?25l[2D[7B[13A⏺[2C[1B⎿[5G Added[12G25[15Glines,[22Gremoved[30G43[33Glines[1B [3G   [38;5;160m[48;5;224m  1 -[38;5;236m# Improvement suggestions (NOT implemented)                                                            [2C[1B[49m[39m [5G [38;5;29m[48;5;194m  1 +[38;5;236m# Improvement suggestions                                                                              [5C[1B[49m[2m  2 [22m [1B[39m     [38;5;160m[48;5;224m  3 -[38;5;236mThese are unrelated observations noticed while repairing the native/external lifecycle                 [49m[39m[K[1B  [6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m  4 -[38;5;236mstatus contract. They are suggestions only; nothing here has been acted on.                            [1B[49m[39m     [38;5;29m[48;5;194m  3 +[38;5;236mUnrelated to the native/external repair. Suggestions only — none of these were implemented.            [49m[39m[K[2C[1B   [38;5;236m[2m  4 [22m [39m[K[2C[1B  [6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m  6 -[38;5;236m## lifecycle.py                                                                                        [5C[1B[38;5;29m[48;5;194m  5 +[38;5;236m## 1. `status()` has no coverage for unspecified owners                                                [2C[1B[49m[39m [5G [38;5;236m[2m  6 [22m [2C[1B[39m [5G [38;5;160m[48;5;224m  8 -[38;5;236m1. **Unknown owners silently fall through to the external result.** `status()` uses a                  [49m[39m[K
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m  9 -[38;5;236m   single `if owner == "native" else ...` expression, so any string other than `"native"`              [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 10 -[38;5;236m   (including typos such as `"Native"`, `""`, or `None`) returns the external value.                   [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 11 -[38;5;236m   Consider an explicit mapping plus a raised `ValueError` (or a documented default) for               [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 12 -[38;5;236m   unrecognised owners, so a misspelled owner fails loudly instead of quietly reporting                [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 13 -[38;5;236m   `"verified"`.                                                                                       [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m  7 +[38;5;236m`requirements.md` names only `native` and `external`. Every other owner falls through to               [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m  8 +[38;5;236m`"bound"`, and no test pins that. During this repair a concurrent edit briefly changed the             [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m  9 +[38;5;236mfallback so that *every* non-native owner returned `"verified"` — tests still passed, because          [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 10 +[38;5;236mnothing asserts the fallback. A test for an unspecified owner would have caught it.                    [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;236m[2m 11[10G[22m [39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 15 -[38;5;236m2. **Status values are bare string literals.** `"platform-pending"` and `"verified"` are               [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 16 -[38;5;236m   duplicated between implementation and tests. Defining them as module-level constants                [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 17 -[38;5;236m   (or an `enum.Enum` / `enum.StrEnum`, stdlib-only) would make a future contract change               [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 18 -[38;5;236m   a one-line edit and prevent drift between code and tests of the kind repaired here.                 [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 12 +[38;5;236m## 2. The contract lives in prose, not in one place the code can reference                             [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;236m[2m 13[10G[22m [39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 20 -[38;5;236m3. **No docstring or type hints.** `status(owner)` has neither. A short docstring naming               [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 21 -[38;5;236m   requirements.md as the source of truth, plus `def status(owner: str) -> str:`, would                [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 22 -[38;5;236m   make the contract discoverable at the call site.                                                    [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 14 +[38;5;236m`requirements.md` states the contract in a sentence, `lifecycle.py` encodes it in branches, and        [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 15 +[38;5;236m`test_lifecycle.py` encodes it a third time in assertions. Three copies drift independently —          [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 16 +[38;5;236mwhich is exactly how the stale `native` assertion survived. A single mapping table (owner →            [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 17 +[38;5;236mstatus) that the tests iterate over would collapse this to one source of truth.                        [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;236m[2m 18[10G[22m [39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 24 -[38;5;236m## test_lifecycle.py                                                                                   [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 19 +[38;5;236m## 3. No signal for an unknown owner                                                                   [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;236m[2m 20[10G[22m [39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 26 -[38;5;236m4. **No coverage for owners outside the two in scope.** There are no tests for unknown,                [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 27 -[38;5;236m   empty, `None`, or differently-cased owner values, which is why suggestion 1's                       [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 28 -[38;5;236m   fall-through behaviour is currently untested and unspecified.                                       [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 21 +[38;5;236m`status()` accepts any string and silently returns `"bound"`. A typo like `"externl"` gets a           [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 22 +[38;5;236mplausible-looking answer instead of an error. Consider raising on unrecognized owners, or              [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 23 +[38;5;236mdocumenting `"bound"` as the deliberate default in `requirements.md`.                                  [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;236m[2m 24[10G[22m [39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 30 -[38;5;236m5. **Assertions duplicate literals rather than referencing a shared contract.** Once                   [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 31 -[38;5;236m   suggestion 2 exists, the tests could assert against the shared constants, or be                     [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 32 -[38;5;236m   table-driven with `subTest` over an owner/expected-status table, so adding a third                  [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 33 -[38;5;236m   owner needs one table row rather than a new method.                                                 [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 25 +[38;5;236m## 4. Contract-history hygiene                                                                         [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;236m[2m 26[10G[22m [39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 35 -[38;5;236m6. **Style: `if __name__ == "__main__": unittest.main()` is on one line.** Minor, but a                [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 36 -[38;5;236m   normal two-line block is the conventional form and is friendlier to diffs.                          [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 27 +[38;5;236m`backlog.md` mixes a superseded technical contract with an unrelated pending business decision         [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 28 +[38;5;236m(D-7 transcription provider). It was useful here as evidence of *which* side was stale, but that       [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 29 +[38;5;236monly worked because someone read it carefully. Superseded contracts would be easier to trust as        [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 30 +[38;5;236mevidence if they carried the date and the change that superseded them.                                 [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;236m[2m 31[10G[22m [39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 38 -[38;5;236m## Tooling / project                                                                                   [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 32 +[38;5;236m## 5. Test file has no runner config                                                                   [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;236m[2m 33[10G[22m [39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 40 -[38;5;236m7. **No automated check that the code matches requirements.md.** The contract lives in                 [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 41 -[38;5;236m   prose while the values live in two Python files, so the two can drift apart silently                [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 42 -[38;5;236m   (exactly the failure repaired here). A lightweight guard - a comment in lifecycle.py                [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 43 -[38;5;236m   citing requirements.md, or a test that reads the requirement line - would catch it.                 [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 44 -[38;5;236m                                                                                                       [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 45 -[38;5;236m8. **No test runner configuration or CI entry point.** The invocation                                  [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 46 -[38;5;236m   `python3 -m unittest test_lifecycle -v` is documented only in README.md prose. A                    [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 47 -[38;5;236m   `Makefile` target or a CI workflow would make the check routine rather than manual.                 [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 48 -[38;5;236m                                                                                                       [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 49 -[38;5;236m9. **No `.gitignore`.** Nothing excludes `__pycache__/` or `*.pyc`, which the test run                 [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 50 -[38;5;236m   generates; they would otherwise show up as untracked noise in `git status`.                         [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 51 -[38;5;236m                                                                                                       [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 52 -[38;5;236m10. **README.md documents two different ways to validate diagnosis.json.** The README                  [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 53 -[38;5;236m    suggests `python3 -c 'import json; json.load(...)'` while the working procedure uses               [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;160m[48;5;224m 54 -[38;5;236m    `python3 -m json.tool diagnosis.json`. Settling on one would avoid ambiguity.                      [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 34 +[38;5;236m`test_lifecycle.py` is run by hand per `README.md`. There is no CI config, no `pytest.ini`, and        [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 35 +[38;5;236mno exit-code-checked entry point, so nothing prevents a stale assertion from sitting unnoticed         [49m[39m
[6G[38;5;29m[48;5;194m 36 +[38;5;236mbetween manual runs — the condition that produced this repair.                                         [49m[39m

